From 0714fc5bb8e9acafcfad4ebc730caa87e7c17fda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 22:42:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] constraints: A window that keeps its shape keeps it in tiles

0033 let a client lock its content to an aspect ratio, but tiles still
stretched it: Mutter's side-by-side tiles and the rectangles a tiling
manager moves a window into filled the whole tile, and the client had
to letterbox inside it. The shape is the app's to choose; tiling must
not override it.

A window is locked when it set a ratio through luma_window_shape_v1,
or, for an X11 client, when WM_NORMAL_HINTS give one aspect ratio
(min_aspect == max_aspect). A range of ratios is a preference and
still fills its tile.

In a tile, side-by-side or external (meta_window_set_externally_tiled),
a locked window takes the largest size with its ratio inside the tile,
centred. When that is below the window's minimum size, the window wins:
the smallest size with its ratio not below its minimum, at the tile's
origin. Maximize uses the same fit; fullscreen is unchanged. While the
person resizes an externally tiled window, the aspect constraint
handles it as for a floating window.

An externally tiled Wayland window keeps the tile as its unconstrained
rectangle when its client commits a smaller size, so a client that
drops its lock fills the tile again.

Marking a window externally tiled, or no longer tiled, now configures
it at once with the new state, even when its frame does not change (a
window already at its shape's size in the tile): before, a queued
move-resize that changed nothing sent no configure, and the client went
on drawing a floating window's shadows. A window put in a tile is
constrained to the rectangle it was asked into; one let go of stays
where it is until the tiling manager moves it.

meta_window_get_frame_rect_in_tile() returns the frame a window takes
in a tile, so a tiling manager can tell that a locked window is where
it put it instead of putting it back.
